%N A342189 Numbers k such that both k and k+1 are not exponentially 2^n-numbers.
%C A342189 The numbers of terms not exceeding 10^k for k = 3, 4, ..., are 8, 76, 775, 7776, 77845, 778303, 7783285, 77832769, ... Apparently this sequence has an asymptotic density 0.0077832... .
%C A342189 The asymptotic density of this sequence is d = 1 - 2 * Product_{p prime} (1 - 1/p^3 + Sum_{k>=2}(1/p^(2^k) - 1/p^(2^k+1))) + Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^3 + 2*Sum_{k>=2} (1/p^(2^k) - 1/p^(2^k+1))) = 0.007783275029795925687866... . The number of terms not exceeding x is d * x + O(x^(1/2+eps)), where eps > 0 (Srichan et al., 2026, a consequence of Theorem 1.1). - _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 11 2026
%H A342189 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A342189/b342189.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A342189 Teerapat Srichan, Nithi Rungtanapirom, and Saeree Wananiyakul, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1051/ita/2026022">On the distribution of consecutive M-free integers</a>, RAIRO - Theoretical Informatics and Applications, Vol. 60 (2026), Article 22.
%e A342189 135 is a term since 135 = 3^3 * 5 and 136 = 2^3 * 17 both have an exponent in their prime factorization which is not a power of 2.
%t A342189 exp2nQ[n_] := AllTrue[FactorInteger[n][[;;, 2]], # == 2^IntegerExponent[#, 2] &]; Select[Range[10^4], ! exp2nQ[#] && ! exp2nQ[# + 1] &]
%o A342189 (PARI) is1(k) = if(k == 1, 0, #select(x -> (x >> valuation(x, 2) > 1), factor(k)[, 2]) > 0);
%o A342189 isok(k) = is1(k) && is1(k+1); \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 11 2026
